WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:05.440
So what is an index?

00:05.480 --> 00:12.920
An index is a data structure that provides a quick access to the rows to improve the speed of your queries.

00:13.000 --> 00:15.960
So an index is like a guide for your database.

00:16.080 --> 00:21.400
In order to speed up the process of searching for data, especially if you have like big tables.

00:21.520 --> 00:23.840
So now in order to understand what are indexes.

00:23.960 --> 00:28.320
Imagine you have a huge book and you want to find a specific topic or chapter.

00:28.320 --> 00:33.680
Instead of flipping a single page in order to find the topic that you are searching for, you would

00:33.680 --> 00:38.520
use the index at the back of the book in order to jump straight to the right page.

00:38.520 --> 00:42.200
And that's exactly what index does, but for your data.

00:42.240 --> 00:48.960
Another analogy that I use in order to understand indexes is think about the indexes as a big hotel.

00:49.000 --> 00:52.000
Now let's say that in the hotel we don't have any guides.

00:52.000 --> 00:56.120
And you would like to find the room number, let's say 501.

00:56.160 --> 01:01.240
Now what you're going to do, you're going to go and search for your room, floor by floor and checking

01:01.240 --> 01:03.480
each room until you find your room.

01:03.480 --> 01:10.120
But instead of that, thankfully hotels have a numbering system and you can ask for a map from the reception

01:10.120 --> 01:14.520
in order to understand in which building in which floor you can find your room.

01:14.520 --> 01:19.440
So by just following the map and maybe some signs, it's going to be very quickly to locate and find

01:19.440 --> 01:21.240
your room in such a big hotel.

01:21.240 --> 01:23.680
And that's exactly what each database needs.

01:23.680 --> 01:30.600
It needs an index in order to help the database finding and locating the right data without having to

01:30.640 --> 01:31.920
scan everything.
